# Configuration for the rtcm-mavlink (user) systemd service.
# Copy to ~/.config/rtcm-mavlink.env and edit for your setup.
#
# RTCM_MAVLINK_ARGS is passed verbatim to the `rtcm-mavlink` CLI. It is
# word-split by systemd, so separate each flag/value with a single space and
# do NOT add surrounding quotes.
#
# RTCM source  (choose ONE): --rtcm-serial <dev> [--baud N] | --rtcm-tcp host:port | --rtcm-file path
# MAVLink dest (required):   --dest udpout:<ip>:<port>
# Optional monitor:          --monitor udpin:0.0.0.0:<port>   (prints RTK verdict to the journal)
#
# The Herelink controller broadcasts MAVLink over its Wi-Fi hotspot. On the
# tested unit the endpoint is 192.168.43.1:14550 (its Android hotspot gateway,
# standard MAVLink/QGC UDP port). If yours differs, connect the Jetson to the
# Herelink Wi-Fi and find it by watching where MAVLink arrives, e.g.:
#   sudo tcpdump -n -i wlan0 udp portrange 14550-14555     # source IP:port = the endpoint
# then set --dest udpout:<that ip>:<that port> and --monitor udpin:0.0.0.0:<that port>.
#
# Example 1 (default) - read RTCM from the local gnss-server relay so the GUI
# can share the same receiver; inject onto the Herelink Wi-Fi stream:
RTCM_MAVLINK_ARGS=--rtcm-tcp 127.0.0.1:50010 --dest udpout:192.168.43.1:14550 --monitor udpin:0.0.0.0:14550

# Example 2 - read the base receiver's USB serial port DIRECTLY (no relay; the
# GUI then cannot use the receiver at the same time):
# RTCM_MAVLINK_ARGS=--rtcm-serial /dev/ttyACM0 --baud 115200 --dest udpout:192.168.43.1:14550 --monitor udpin:0.0.0.0:14550
